The Long Game =C2=B7 Strategy weekly =C2=B7 Issue 80

MIDDLE POWERS AND THE ART OF NOT CHOOSING

A useful habit when reading the news: count the governments that refuse to pi=
ck a side and are rewarded for it.

India buys discounted oil from one camp and joins naval exercises with the ot=
her. The Gulf states host American bases, sell energy to China, and broker ta=
lks no one else can. Turkey sits inside NATO and outside its consensus. Indon=
esia, Brazil and South Africa each have their own versions.

This used to be called non-alignment and was treated as a stance of weakness.=
 Today it looks more like arbitrage. When two large powers compete for partne=
rs, the partners can charge for their attention: technology transfer, investm=
ent, arms on better terms, a quieter voice on human rights.

Three implications follow.

1. Coalitions will be thinner and more transactional. Expect "minilaterals" b=
uilt around a single task (undersea cables, critical minerals, a shipping lan=
e) instead of grand alliances.
2. The price of loyalty rises. Any great power that asks for exclusivity will=
 have to pay for it, and both are reluctant.
3. Arbitrage has limits. If competition turns into crisis, fence-sitters lose=
 leverage fast because the large powers stop bidding and start demanding.

The comfortable middle exists because the competition is still managed. Watch=
 the hedgers. When they start choosing, the era has changed.

Reading this week
- A long profile of Gulf sovereign wealth funds' Asia strategy
- An old essay on Nehru's non-alignment, which reads differently now

